1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,630
Every piece of a building is designed to

2
00:00:02,670 --> 00:00:03,910
carry a number.

3
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:04,790
A beam.

4
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:05,750
A bolt.

5
00:00:05,790 --> 00:00:06,870
A weld.

6
00:00:06,910 --> 00:00:09,110
Each one has a load it's rated for,

7
00:00:09,150 --> 00:00:11,470
with a margin on top, in case the world

8
00:00:11,510 --> 00:00:14,120
turns out heavier than anyone expected.

9
00:00:14,892 --> 00:00:18,212
On a Friday evening in July 1981,

10
00:00:18,252 --> 00:00:20,292
in a hotel in Kansas City,

11
00:00:20,332 --> 00:00:22,852
one small steel connection was holding up

12
00:00:22,892 --> 00:00:25,012
two walkways full of people.

13
00:00:25,052 --> 00:00:26,852
It had never been strong enough to meet

14
00:00:26,892 --> 00:00:28,052
the building code.

15
00:00:28,092 --> 00:00:30,532
And months before, a change on a drawing

16
00:00:30,572 --> 00:00:32,372
had doubled what it was being asked to

17
00:00:32,412 --> 00:00:33,462
carry.

18
00:00:34,267 --> 00:00:36,147
At about five past seven,

19
00:00:36,187 --> 00:00:38,037
it reached its limit.

20
00:00:45,183 --> 00:00:47,783
The Hyatt Regency Kansas City opened in

21
00:00:47,823 --> 00:00:49,623
July 1980.

22
00:00:49,663 --> 00:00:52,263
Its centrepiece was a vast atrium,

23
00:00:52,303 --> 00:00:55,463
a glass-roofed lobby several storeys high.

24
00:00:55,503 --> 00:00:57,463
And crossing it, in mid-air,

25
00:00:57,503 --> 00:00:59,943
were three suspended walkways.

26
00:00:59,983 --> 00:01:02,076
The second-floor walkway hung directly

27
00:01:02,116 --> 00:01:04,370
beneath the one on the fourth floor.

28
00:01:04,410 --> 00:01:06,663
The third-floor walkway ran on its own,

29
00:01:06,703 --> 00:01:08,553
off to one side.

30
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:12,110
The walkways didn't rest on columns.

31
00:01:12,150 --> 00:01:14,430
They hung from the roof on slender steel

32
00:01:14,470 --> 00:01:16,910
rods, so they seemed to float across the

33
00:01:16,950 --> 00:01:17,710
room.

34
00:01:17,750 --> 00:01:19,310
People loved them.

35
00:01:19,350 --> 00:01:21,230
From up there, you could look straight

36
00:01:21,270 --> 00:01:23,440
down onto the lobby floor.

37
00:01:24,142 --> 00:01:26,182
On Friday evenings that summer,

38
00:01:26,222 --> 00:01:29,062
the hotel held a tea dance in the atrium.

39
00:01:29,102 --> 00:01:30,782
On July the seventeenth,

40
00:01:30,822 --> 00:01:33,462
around sixteen hundred people came.

41
00:01:33,502 --> 00:01:35,662
Dozens of them stood on the walkways,

42
00:01:35,702 --> 00:01:37,432
watching the dancers below.

43
00:01:40,808 --> 00:01:42,848
To understand what happened next,

44
00:01:42,888 --> 00:01:45,938
you only need to understand one joint.

45
00:01:46,642 --> 00:01:49,162
Each walkway was carried by cross-beams

46
00:01:49,202 --> 00:01:50,842
called box beams.

47
00:01:50,882 --> 00:01:53,642
Each box beam was made from two C-shaped

48
00:01:53,682 --> 00:01:56,402
steel channels, welded edge to edge to

49
00:01:56,442 --> 00:01:58,069
form a hollow box.

50
00:01:58,109 --> 00:02:00,362
A steel rod passed through a hole in that

51
00:02:00,402 --> 00:02:01,162
box.

52
00:02:01,202 --> 00:02:03,762
Underneath, a nut and a washer held the

53
00:02:03,802 --> 00:02:04,682
beam up.

54
00:02:04,722 --> 00:02:06,762
All the weight of that section of walkway

55
00:02:06,802 --> 00:02:09,372
pressed down onto that one nut.

56
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:12,540
The original design was simple.

57
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:15,340
One long, continuous rod would run from

58
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:17,260
the roof, through the fourth-floor

59
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:19,420
walkway, and straight on down to the

60
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:21,020
second-floor walkway.

61
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:23,780
Each nut carried only one walkway.

62
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:26,007
The rod itself carried both,

63
00:02:26,047 --> 00:02:28,750
and steel rods are very good at that.

64
00:02:29,475 --> 00:02:32,115
But even this design had a problem.

65
00:02:32,155 --> 00:02:34,555
When federal investigators later analysed

66
00:02:34,595 --> 00:02:36,715
it, they concluded that the connection

67
00:02:36,755 --> 00:02:38,635
would have reached only about sixty

68
00:02:38,675 --> 00:02:41,115
percent of the capacity the Kansas City

69
00:02:41,155 --> 00:02:42,955
building code expected.

70
00:02:42,995 --> 00:02:44,555
Before anything changed,

71
00:02:44,595 --> 00:02:47,005
it was already under-rated.

72
00:02:50,308 --> 00:02:52,988
A single continuous rod has a practical

73
00:02:53,028 --> 00:02:53,948
problem.

74
00:02:53,988 --> 00:02:56,268
It's long, and the nut for the fourth

75
00:02:56,308 --> 00:02:58,108
floor has to sit somewhere in the middle

76
00:02:58,148 --> 00:02:58,988
of it.

77
00:02:59,028 --> 00:03:01,148
Getting it there, and building the walkway

78
00:03:01,188 --> 00:03:03,998
around it, is awkward work.

79
00:03:04,725 --> 00:03:07,245
So during construction, the detail was

80
00:03:07,285 --> 00:03:08,085
changed.

81
00:03:08,125 --> 00:03:10,685
Instead of one rod, there would be two.

82
00:03:10,725 --> 00:03:12,925
The first ran from the roof down to the

83
00:03:12,965 --> 00:03:14,552
fourth-floor box beam.

84
00:03:14,592 --> 00:03:17,405
A second, separate rod hung from that same

85
00:03:17,445 --> 00:03:19,325
box beam, down to the second-floor

86
00:03:19,365 --> 00:03:20,205
walkway.

87
00:03:20,245 --> 00:03:22,925
On paper, the two arrangements look almost

88
00:03:22,965 --> 00:03:24,335
identical.

89
00:03:25,058 --> 00:03:26,778
But follow the load.

90
00:03:26,818 --> 00:03:29,338
The second-floor walkway no longer hangs

91
00:03:29,378 --> 00:03:30,378
from the roof.

92
00:03:30,418 --> 00:03:32,978
It hangs from the fourth-floor walkway.

93
00:03:33,018 --> 00:03:35,578
So the nut under the fourth-floor box beam

94
00:03:35,618 --> 00:03:37,418
now carries the fourth floor,

95
00:03:37,458 --> 00:03:39,948
and everything hanging beneath it.

96
00:03:40,642 --> 00:03:43,002
Picture two climbers on a rope.

97
00:03:43,042 --> 00:03:44,442
Each one holds the rope,

98
00:03:44,482 --> 00:03:47,002
and each grip carries one person.

99
00:03:47,042 --> 00:03:49,189
Now the lower climber lets go,

100
00:03:49,229 --> 00:03:51,612
and holds on to the upper climber's ankles

101
00:03:51,882 --> 00:03:52,562
instead.

102
00:03:52,602 --> 00:03:54,655
Nothing about the rope has changed.

103
00:03:54,695 --> 00:03:56,522
But the upper climber's grip is now

104
00:03:56,562 --> 00:03:58,492
holding two people.

105
00:03:59,183 --> 00:04:01,490
That was the fourth-floor connection.

106
00:04:01,530 --> 00:04:04,103
A joint that was already under-rated was

107
00:04:04,143 --> 00:04:06,633
now being asked to carry roughly twice the

108
00:04:06,783 --> 00:04:07,513
load.

109
00:04:08,225 --> 00:04:11,385
So how did a change like that get through?

110
00:04:11,425 --> 00:04:13,985
The steel fabricator proposed the two-rod

111
00:04:14,025 --> 00:04:16,265
detail and prepared the shop drawings.

112
00:04:16,305 --> 00:04:18,265
Those drawings went back to the structural

113
00:04:18,305 --> 00:04:20,345
engineers, and returned with their review

114
00:04:20,385 --> 00:04:21,225
stamp.

115
00:04:21,265 --> 00:04:22,745
What was said along the way,

116
00:04:22,785 --> 00:04:25,065
including a disputed phone call,

117
00:04:25,105 --> 00:04:27,185
became one of the most argued-over

118
00:04:27,225 --> 00:04:29,225
questions in engineering ethics.

119
00:04:29,265 --> 00:04:31,585
What the investigations made clear is that

120
00:04:31,625 --> 00:04:33,705
the connection was never checked against

121
00:04:33,745 --> 00:04:35,195
the doubled load.

122
00:04:35,892 --> 00:04:38,412
And there had been a warning sign.

123
00:04:38,452 --> 00:04:40,572
While the hotel was still being built,

124
00:04:40,612 --> 00:04:43,052
part of the atrium roof collapsed.

125
00:04:43,092 --> 00:04:44,599
Nobody was killed.

126
00:04:44,639 --> 00:04:46,225
The engineers investigated,

127
00:04:46,265 --> 00:04:48,052
and assured the owner of the atrium's

128
00:04:48,092 --> 00:04:48,972
safety.

129
00:04:49,012 --> 00:04:51,252
The flaw in the walkway connections was

130
00:04:51,292 --> 00:04:52,702
not caught.

131
00:04:55,975 --> 00:04:58,175
Friday, July the seventeenth,

132
00:04:58,215 --> 00:04:59,775
1981.

133
00:04:59,815 --> 00:05:01,535
The atrium is full.

134
00:05:01,575 --> 00:05:03,775
There's music, and there are people on the

135
00:05:03,815 --> 00:05:06,122
floor, and on the walkways above.

136
00:05:06,162 --> 00:05:08,095
The fourth-floor walkway is carrying its

137
00:05:08,135 --> 00:05:10,535
own crowd, and the entire second-floor

138
00:05:10,575 --> 00:05:13,505
walkway below it, with its crowd too.

139
00:05:14,225 --> 00:05:16,665
At about five past seven in the evening,

140
00:05:16,705 --> 00:05:18,665
at the fourth-floor box beams,

141
00:05:18,705 --> 00:05:20,852
the welded seams split.

142
00:05:20,892 --> 00:05:22,745
The nuts and washers pulled straight

143
00:05:22,785 --> 00:05:24,105
through the steel.

144
00:05:24,145 --> 00:05:26,425
The fourth-floor walkway dropped onto the

145
00:05:26,465 --> 00:05:28,905
second-floor walkway, and both fell onto

146
00:05:28,945 --> 00:05:31,035
the crowded lobby below.

147
00:05:36,250 --> 00:05:39,010
One hundred and fourteen people died.

148
00:05:39,050 --> 00:05:41,490
Two hundred and sixteen were injured.

149
00:05:41,530 --> 00:05:43,890
Rescuers worked through the night to reach

150
00:05:43,930 --> 00:05:46,050
those trapped beneath the wreckage.

151
00:05:46,090 --> 00:05:48,290
It was the deadliest structural collapse

152
00:05:48,330 --> 00:05:51,700
in American history, until 2001.

153
00:05:54,975 --> 00:05:57,015
The National Bureau of Standards,

154
00:05:57,055 --> 00:06:00,055
known today as NIST, led the federal

155
00:06:00,095 --> 00:06:01,495
investigation.

156
00:06:01,535 --> 00:06:03,775
Its engineers recovered the hanger rods

157
00:06:03,815 --> 00:06:06,215
and box beams, and load-tested matching

158
00:06:06,255 --> 00:06:08,162
connections in the laboratory.

159
00:06:08,202 --> 00:06:10,215
Their conclusion was stark.

160
00:06:10,255 --> 00:06:12,135
The load on the connection at the moment

161
00:06:12,175 --> 00:06:14,535
of collapse was only about thirty-one

162
00:06:14,575 --> 00:06:16,615
percent of what a connection designed to

163
00:06:16,655 --> 00:06:18,855
the Kansas City code should have been able

164
00:06:18,895 --> 00:06:20,105
to hold.

165
00:06:20,808 --> 00:06:23,408
In other words, this was not a structure

166
00:06:23,448 --> 00:06:26,448
pushed past its rating by a freak event.

167
00:06:26,488 --> 00:06:29,008
Its rating had been quietly cut,

168
00:06:29,048 --> 00:06:31,778
long before the crowd ever arrived.

169
00:06:35,058 --> 00:06:38,058
So who decided, and who paid?

170
00:06:38,098 --> 00:06:40,418
The change made one detail simpler to

171
00:06:40,458 --> 00:06:42,218
fabricate and install.

172
00:06:42,258 --> 00:06:44,405
That was the entire upside.

173
00:06:44,445 --> 00:06:46,818
The downside was a wave of lawsuits from

174
00:06:46,858 --> 00:06:48,698
victims and their families.

175
00:06:48,738 --> 00:06:51,098
Missouri's licensing authorities found the

176
00:06:51,138 --> 00:06:52,938
engineer of record and the project

177
00:06:52,978 --> 00:06:55,151
engineer grossly negligent.

178
00:06:55,191 --> 00:06:57,018
Both lost their engineering licences in

179
00:06:57,058 --> 00:06:59,298
Missouri, and their firm lost its

180
00:06:59,338 --> 00:07:01,098
certificate to practise engineering in the

181
00:07:01,138 --> 00:07:02,188
state.

182
00:07:02,892 --> 00:07:05,572
Engineering ethics courses still debate

183
00:07:05,612 --> 00:07:07,782
how the responsibility should have been

184
00:07:07,972 --> 00:07:08,479
shared.

185
00:07:08,519 --> 00:07:10,452
But the lesson the profession took was

186
00:07:10,492 --> 00:07:11,252
blunt.

187
00:07:11,292 --> 00:07:14,422
If your stamp is on it, it's yours.

188
00:07:17,725 --> 00:07:20,485
After Kansas City, the industry tightened

189
00:07:20,525 --> 00:07:22,645
how connection designs and shop drawings

190
00:07:22,685 --> 00:07:23,845
are reviewed.

191
00:07:23,885 --> 00:07:26,405
Today, a change like that two-rod detail

192
00:07:26,445 --> 00:07:27,925
is supposed to trigger a fresh

193
00:07:27,965 --> 00:07:30,512
calculation, not just a stamp.

194
00:07:30,552 --> 00:07:32,485
The Hyatt Regency is now taught in

195
00:07:32,525 --> 00:07:34,485
engineering ethics classes across the

196
00:07:34,525 --> 00:07:35,952
United States.

197
00:07:35,992 --> 00:07:38,138
Not because the physics is complicated.

198
00:07:38,178 --> 00:07:40,565
It's one rod, two nuts, and a load that

199
00:07:40,605 --> 00:07:41,525
doubled.

200
00:07:41,565 --> 00:07:43,925
It's taught because the number was always

201
00:07:43,965 --> 00:07:45,655
there to be checked.

202
00:07:46,350 --> 00:07:48,790
Every structure around you was built to

203
00:07:48,830 --> 00:07:50,190
carry a number.

204
00:07:50,230 --> 00:07:52,310
Most of the time, there's a margin on top

205
00:07:52,350 --> 00:07:54,150
of it that you'll never see.

206
00:07:54,190 --> 00:07:56,390
The question is always the same.

207
00:07:56,430 --> 00:07:59,640
How far past the rating can it really go?

208
00:08:02,725 --> 00:08:04,765
If you want the next investigation,

209
00:08:04,805 --> 00:08:05,805
subscribe.

210
00:08:05,845 --> 00:08:07,935
There's a new one every week.

